WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.473
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.473 --> 00:00:10.135
I've been in a senior operations role
at my company for a number of years and

00:00:10.135 --> 00:00:14.030
I wanted a way to kind of codify
what I've learned on the job.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.290
What is this COO do?

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:21.150
How far off the mark is my capability
are there things I can learn?

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:25.710
So I was really interested being
an operations professional trying to

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:30.360
understand what witness to some
like Stanford is defining COO as.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.320
&gt;&gt; I think what drew me to it
specifically was the bringing a strategic

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.740
element to the COO role.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.910
And having that kind of be at
the heart of Silicon Valley.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:45.160
&gt;&gt; I wanted to work with Stanford
faculty and kind of move beyond all

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.730
the readings that I had been doing,
case studies that I had been looking at.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.275
&gt;&gt; I thought it was a perfect opportunity.

00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:54.485
For me and us as a company to invest in,

00:00:54.485 --> 00:00:57.735
continue to elevate the COO
role within our company.

00:00:57.735 --> 00:00:59.965
&gt;&gt; You really do owe it to yourself and

00:00:59.965 --> 00:01:05.330
to your staff to put new information
in to be influenced by new thinking.

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:10.080
&gt;&gt; It's important for you to get to
maximize the value of the investment

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:17.080
you're making to take it seriously and
really dive in so you're back in school.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.950
&gt;&gt; It was really important for
me to step outside my context.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:25.830
And taking something new to be able to
come to the table with new modalities,

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.070
new thinking and new approaches.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:32.880
&gt;&gt; There is a lot of opportunity to learn,
to network, to create relationships,

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.530
both with our peers in the classroom
as well as the faculty at Stanford.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.670
&gt;&gt; You're looking for the things
that you can't get out of a book,

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.200
that you can't get out of a podcast.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:41.950
It's the people.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:43.100
It's the faculty.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.889
It's the connections that you build that
really make this program worthwhile.

00:01:46.889 --> 00:01:51.282
[MUSIC]

